## How to Make “Time‑Freeze” a Character Ability in Mugen  

Below is a **road‑map + starter script** for a character that can toggle a *time‑pause* effect and immediately fire a special attack when the pause is active.

* Works for **Mugen 3** (the MGEY‑based engine).  
* You can translate any placeholder messages to your own character’s style.  
* Feel free to add your own graphics, sounds or extra logic.

---

### 1️⃣  Choose a control to invoke the pause  

| Input | How to bind it in Mugen | Typical cmdl | Example “pause” event |
|-------|--------------------------|-------------|-----------------------|
|  **F** (default) | Controller A btn2 (A‑A‑A‑A) | `c_input.k सपف` or `c_event_state=11` | `playerState->input(attack) = true` |
| **CTRL** | Controller B btn‑B | `c_input.k ct` | `input('c')` |
| **Super‑Adv** (if you have a “Super Advance” mode) | `c_event_state=8` | — |

> **Tip** – Add a *static* controller mapping file (e.g. `data/gameCommand.cmd` or `gameCommand.cfg`) that gives you a clean, well‑named name such as `pause`, `freeze`, `ff` or `st`.  The script then looks for `game->input("pause")`.

---

### 2️⃣  The “freeze” effect – pause the system’s timers  

In Mugen the `time` variable **resets** to zero when a move’s `startTime` or `stopTime` is set.  By calling the same routine before and after your special attack you can *lock* the game’s timer for a few frames.

```cpp
// File: freeze.cpp
//-------------------------------------------------
#include <vm/gp.h>   // for gm_global, g_setTime
#include <vm/gui.h>   // for g_putStr, g_drawLine
#include "cmdline.h"  // for input()
extern gint game_players;

void GameEffect_G()                // “Game” script – plain C++
{
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 1) Check for pause command on every frame
    if (input("pause"))
    {
        // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 2) Let's pause the game’s timeline.
        //    gSetTime(0) runs after the current step – so the effect really
        //    begins on the *next* frame.
        gSetTime(0);                     // stops all timer updates

        // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 3) Pause UI (optional) – freeze the in‑game clock
        gPutStr( 0x43, 0x00, "  TIME  X FROZEN  "   );
        gPauseDraw();                   // stops the in‑game timer drawing

        // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 4) Save time offset so we can resume it later
        static int time_offset = 0;
        time_offset = gState.TimerCount + 1;   // remember where we were

        // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 5) Prevent every normal move from being able to move any more.
        //    (If you want the opponent to be stunned together, set a flag here.)
        if (game_players > 1)   // multiplayer
            gameData->TimeImmobilised = true;
        else
            gameData->TimeImmobilised = true; // SNG

        // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 6) Wait a short moment (e.g. 5 frames) before resuming.
        Waitio_TimerMore(5);

        // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 7) Freeze animation – show a “frozen” sprite for a few frames
        //    (you can use onlyHitBitmap, onlyRender, or a custom custom bitmap)
        gDrawBitmapOnly(game->GetGraphics(), Timer, onlyHitBitmap);
        Waitio_TimerMore(frameSpr);

        // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
        // 8) Clean‑up – restore the timers and disable the UI
        gState.TimerCount = time_offset;
        gState.DisablePause = true;
        gPauseDraw();
    }

    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 9) ALL MOVE COUNTERS – here is where your **special ability** is fired.
    if (input("special") ) {
        // (see Section 3) – “special” entry also disables the pause.
        SpecialAbility();
    }

    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 10) If you want the opponent to be frozen while you talk, you can set a
    //     flag here and make the opponent’s MoveOn time field 0.
    // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
}
```

**Explanation of the key parts**

| Line | What it does |
|------|--------------|
| `if (input("pause")) {}` | Detects every frame whether the player pressed the “Pause” button. |
| `gSetTime(0);` | Global timer callback – stops the native system‑clock updates. |
| `gPutStr(0x43,0x00,"TIME X FROZEN")` | Simple chat‑box message to tell everybody “time is stopped”. |
| `gPauseDraw();` | Stops everything that depends on `FrameCount` or `Timer` (the in‑game countdown, health bars, etc.). |
| `Waitio_TimerMore(5);` | Forces the same Freeze function to be called again **only** 5 times later → gives a *visible* “timer‑paused” period. |
| `gameData->TimeImmobilised = true;` | A tiny “global” flag that can later be used to stop all move counter updates. |
| `gState.DisablePause = true;` | Hides the UI again once we’re ready. |
| `SpecialAbility();` | Your **target** routine that implements the very‑last attack. (You’ll see an example below.) |

> **Pro tip:** If you need the pause to *last* for a fixed number of moves (instead of a fixed number of frames), use `Waitio_TimerMore(N)` instead of `Waitio_TimerMore(0)` and let the loop iterate.

---

### 3️⃣  The “special ability” – what actually happens when time is frozen  

Place all logic for your **last aerial / parry / light‑bomb** in a *GameEffect* called `Spr` (the entry is entered with a special button).  Here is a minimal skeleton that you can flesh out.

```cpp
// File: spr.cpp   –  Speechily‑named "Special" entry
// ----------------------------------------------------
#include <vm/gp.h>   // gm_global
#include "cmdline.h" // input()
extern gint game_players;

void Spr()               // entry for the “special” button
{
    // ----------------------------------------------------
    // 1)  First the pause button forces a call to this same routine
    // ----------------------------------------------------
    SpecialAbility();
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// our specialised function runs when the “special” button is pressed
void SpecialAbility()
{
    // ----------------------------------------------------
    // 2)  Make sure we don't fall back into the normal timing
    // ----------------------------------------------------
    StopMoveOnTimer();

    // ----------------------------------------------------
    // 3)  Prevent opponent from moving – optional nuke
    // ----------------------------------------------------
    if (game_players > 1)   // multiplayer
        gameData->OpponentFrozen = true;
    else
        gameData->OpponentFrozen = true; // SNG

